A box holds $2$ red counters and $2$ blue counters, so red and blue are equally likely on each draw. A counter is drawn and its colour recorded, the counter is returned, and a second counter is drawn. Find the probability that both counters are blue, as a fraction in simplest form.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (F1) at (2.2,1.8);
\coordinate (F2) at (2.2,0);
\coordinate (F3) at (2.2,-1.8);
\coordinate (E1) at (4.8,2.4);
\coordinate (E2) at (4.8,1.2);
\coordinate (E3) at (4.8,0.6);
\coordinate (E4) at (4.8,-0.6);
\coordinate (E5) at (4.8,-1.2);
\coordinate (E6) at (4.8,-2.4);
\draw (O)--(F1) node[midway,above,font=\small] {$R_{1}$};
\draw (O)--(F2) node[midway,fill=white,inner sep=1pt,font=\small] {$R_{2}$};
\draw (O)--(F3) node[midway,below,font=\small] {$B$};
\draw (F1)--(E1) node[midway,above,font=\small] {$R_{2}$};
\draw (F1)--(E2) node[midway,below,font=\small] {$B$};
\draw (F2)--(E3) node[midway,above,font=\small] {$R_{1}$};
\draw (F2)--(E4) node[midway,below,font=\small] {$B$};
\draw (F3)--(E5) node[midway,above,font=\small] {$R_{1}$};
\draw (F3)--(E6) node[midway,below,font=\small] {$R_{2}$};
\node[right,font=\small] at (E1) {$R_{1}R_{2}$};
\node[right,font=\small] at (E2) {$R_{1}B$};
\node[right,font=\small] at (E3) {$R_{2}R_{1}$};
\node[right,font=\small] at (E4) {$R_{2}B$};
\node[right,font=\small] at (E5) {$BR_{1}$};
\node[right,font=\small] at (E6) {$BR_{2}$};
\node[font=\small] at (2.2,3.2) {Draw $1$};
\node[font=\small] at (4.8,3.2) {Draw $2$};
\node[font=\small] at (6.4,3.2) {Outcome};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
A box holds three counters: two red ones labelled $R_{1}$ and $R_{2}$, and one blue one labelled $B$. One counter is drawn and kept, then a second counter is drawn. Because each counter is equally likely to be picked at each draw, the six paths of the tree are equally likely. Find the probability that both counters are red, as a fraction in simplest form.

